body:
Adding to the list of c++20 features that are useful, I find [designated initializers](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/aggregate_initialization#Designated_initializers) very handy.

e.g. in a test that generates a struct, the test instances look like this:
	CHECK_PARSE_OK("+10", (FormatSpecPart{ .width = 10, .sign_type = FormatSpec::SIGN_ALWAYS }));
	CHECK_PARSE_OK("<+10", (FormatSpecPart{ .width = 10, .align_type = FormatSpec::ALIGN_LEFT, .sign_type = FormatSpec::SIGN_ALWAYS }));
	CHECK_PARSE_OK("#10", (FormatSpecPart{ .width = 10, .alternate_form = true }));
	CHECK_PARSE_OK("+#10", (FormatSpecPart{ .width = 10, .sign_type = FormatSpec::SIGN_ALWAYS, .alternate_form = true }));
	CHECK_PARSE_OK("<+#10", (FormatSpecPart{ .width = 10, .align_type = FormatSpec::ALIGN_LEFT, .sign_type = FormatSpec::SIGN_ALWAYS, .alternate_form = true }));

There are a dozen fields in that struct, so being able to use named fields to explicitly populate just some of them is very handy.
